riot = "If you're new to 2xko, or fighting games in general, you might have had the following experience. You do the tutorial to learn how to play. Jump into a match, and your opponent starts attacking you. Not able to remember everything on the spot, you end up randomly pressing buttons, hoping for a hit to land. Whether you end up winning or losing, you're not sure why. Then you realize there are people who seem to really know what they're doing, and the voice in your head starts telling you that you are bad at fighting games and don't belong here. It may be counterintuitive, but knowing you're bad at fighting games actually makes you fit right in with everyone else in the fighting game community. In FGC language, someone who is defeated easily is called free. And it comes from the arcade days where if you could beat your opponent, you could keep playing for free, while the loser had to pay more coins to try again. Of course, no one wants to be easy to beat, but everyone, including the best players, experience what it's like to be free. And this shared experience has become a ritual. At the end of each evo, they play a clip of a classic Hong Kong film where the hero releases the prisoners and says, you're all free now. Yeah, it's a funny meme at this point, but the deeper meaning behind it is that knowing you're free means you have the humility to learn and get better. The very act of watching this video right now means you have this mindset, and I'm going to show you the one thing you need to know that will unlock an entire genre's worth of strategy. It's going to sound crazy, but that one thing is blocking. Blocking is as fundamental to defense in fighting games as walls are to first person shooters. The problem is that blocking is not intuitive in the way hiding behind a box is in Valorant. We've all played hiding games as a child, but not every child has learned how to block overheads. But blocking is not exciting. An Australian fighting game player who was being coached half jokingly said, I didn't pay $60 to block. There's a lot of good news, however. 2xko is free to play and thus free to block. And the concept of blocking only takes three minutes to learn. The best part about learning how to block is that it will open up an entire new genre of gaming for you. And earning your wins with these strategies in a fighting game is one of the most rewarding gaming experiences you can have. The best part is you already know about movement and attacking from other games. So blocking is just one more thing. After watching this video, you'll start to see the strategy in 2x KO matches and hopefully apply them yourself. Ready? Let's go. Blocking in a fighting game allows you to take a hit without suffering any damage or minimal damage depending on the blocked attack. The blocking system in 2xko has been in fighting games since the 80s and hasn't really changed much and the game even still uses the eight directional control scheme. The command is hold back to block. This phrase has become a sort of secret handshake or even a shibboleth in fighting game circles because it is unique to fighting games and it indicates knowledge of how to play without random button mashing. But Note that in 2x KO it will instruct you to hold away instead of back. It's the same concept, just different terminology. If you ever need a glossary for fighting games, the one by INFIL is the gold standard. So on the eight way controller, hold a way to block while standing. This is called high block. And yeah, your champion will walk backwards. Don't be alarmed. Hold down away to block crouching. This is called low block. And in the air you can hold any away direction to air block. Now note that the direction to hold away flips when your opponent is on the other side. And this matters because side switching happens suddenly and often and on purpose by attackers to make it harder to block. So why are there three types of blocking? Because it allows there to be different types of attacks, each one beating certain types of blocking but not others. Every champion has three different attack types which are high, mid and low. To identify them, high attacks are usually attacks from the air. Mid and low attacks are usually from the ground, and low attacks are usually crouching attack that look like they are aiming for your legs or ankles. To know for sure, you can check in training mode, high block defends against high and mid attacks but loses to low attacks. The logic is that your legs are vulnerable while standing. Low block defends against low and mid attacks but loses to high attacks. The logic is that you're not guarding above your head when crouching. Air block defends against all three high, mid and low attacks, but you have to be in the air to do it. The logic is. I don't know. This is just video game logic. The general blocking strategy is to high block when your opponent is in the air and low block when they're on the ground. But a good blocker can be like a turtle. So they gave everyone a move that beats all forms of blocking called Throw. Throw can be done in the air or on the ground, but you must be close to your opponent to do it. If not, you'll just grab nothing and look like a fool. But if both players do a throw command around the same time, it will cancel each other out in what's called Throw Escape. And no one gets hurt. And that's it. As promised, three minutes to show you what blocking is. Now, by itself, this might feel like an overcomplicated version of Rock Paper Scissors. So we need to combine this with attacking and movement. When it comes to movement, it's not all that different from other games. Dashing is a burst of speed. Jumping makes you go high and far. Super Jumping makes you go higher and further. What might feel weird at first is that jumping trajectories are fixed. Only super jumps let you drift in the air. Also, some privileged champions can dash in the air. The most important things to know for 2xko is that there's a tiny window where you cannot block while dashing. There's a tiny window where you cannot block when starting a jump. People get hit here a lot when trying to jump away from an attack, so be careful. Lastly, jumping will dodge ground throws. Hit the fool on the way down. That's it for movement. Now for the fun part. Attacking. In League of Legends or Valorant, you can move and attack even while getting hit. Hit. It's almost like they don't feel pain. But in 2xko, getting hit clearly puts your champion in a lot of pain. Just look at the animation. During this pain animation, you cannot attack, cannot move, cannot tag, and cannot block until the animation finishes. You're momentarily stunned, which is why this state is called Hitstun. If you get hit again while in Hitstun, then it's called a combo. And. And this counter pops up, counting how many hits there are in a row. The only way to break out of a combo is when your break meter is full and you spend it. It's kinda like a get out of Jail Free card, so use it wisely. Without it, you cannot control your champion until the combo finishes. It's like watching an unskippable cutscene of you getting beaten. Because almost any hit can lead into a long and damaging combo, you really do not want to get hit. So why do people get hit in the first place? Often because they are doing an attack or special move, and you cannot block while doing those. But the only way to see what's really going on is is to go frame by frame. Historically, fighting games were played at 60 frames per second, so one frame is measured as 1/60 of a second. Every attack animation has three parts that each last for a certain number of frames. The first part is just winding up for the attack so it doesn't do any harm. The second part is where contact is made and damage done. And the third part is a follow through animation which also does no damage. These are called startup, active and recovery frames, and it kind of reflects how martial arts work in real life. Nobody can punch, kick or slash without startup frames because that would be superhuman and look super weird because you cannot block during an attacking animation. The startup and recovery frames are great windows of opportunities to hit someone, but they are tiny windows, so timing is everything. A difference of a couple of frames can determine who gets to do their combo and whole who has to watch the unskippable cutscene. Here's why. If two Dariuses do the same attack at the exact same time, both will trade hits and both will be in hitstun. So no one gets comboed. But say Pink Darius presses the button one frame sooner. He then completely wins the exchange and puts Blue Darius in a devastating combo. If Pink Darius is one frame slower, then he completely loses the exchange. But here it just seems like a game of reactions. So where is the strategy? The strategy is to press the attack button with the faster animation or less startup frames. If Pink Darius pressed the light attack button instead of heavy here, his active frame would come out seven frames faster than Blue Darius. Even if Pink Darius pressed the button one frame later, or even up to six frames later, Pink would still completely win the exchange. So the strategy just to use the fastest attack to increase the chance of winning. Winning these exchanges in this case, sure. But while speed is important, it doesn't matter if the attack doesn't reach. If both Dariuses were a little further apart and Pink Darius did his faster light attack, Blue Darius would win simply because the heavy attack has more reach and it will hit Pink Darius during his attack's recovery frames. The general rule of thumb is that faster attacks are shorter range, while slower attacks are longer range. The three normal attack buttons are light, medium and heavy, but you can also kind of see them as fast, medium, slow and short, medium long. The general strategy is to use fast attacks when close, and longer attacks when further away. But in the examples I gave, it assumes both players are ready to attack each other at the same time or close to the same time. In a real match with moving champions, you will be caught off guard at one point. You will know their attack is already coming and any attack you do will lose out because it'll be too late or not enough reach. This is where you bust out the fastest move in the block. Even the fastest attacks have startup frames. Blocking has no startup frames because it is instantaneous block when you cannot escape and when you know your attack will likely lose to their attack. It's actually not that different from taking cover behind a wall when caught off guard in an FPS. But in fighting games you can pull up that wall immediately. But while blocking is better than getting hit, you will now be stuck in a blocking state called Blockstun where you cannot move, attack or tag for a brief moment. Blocking a series of attacks will keep you in blockstun and in 2x ko, this will happen a lot. However, this is not an unskippable cutscene. You can still do like four things. Switch between high block and low block. Push away your opponent with push assist. Try to get away yourself with retreating guard and if you have it, break. These defensive mechanics will save your life, but you will not be able to block everything and you will eventually get hit and knocked down. In a knockdown state, you also cannot move, attack or tag, and it starts from the hit or throw that knocked you down and ends when you get up again. But the knockdown states are more merciful because you are invincible for much of its duration, which is indicated by this white outline on your champion. But your opponent will not be merciful and will be ready to hit you as soon as you get up again. They can even time their attack, so their active frames hit you the moment your attack starts, guaranteeing you will lose the exchange. Luckily, you can choose one of three locations to get up to be a harder target. And there is the get up attack option that has invincible startup frames. But be careful, it also has lots of recovery frames, leaving you vulnerable. One last note is that knockdown states can happen in the air too, also with multiple positioning options. So now I've described the four states you can be in that does not let you freely move or attack. Basically the ones that make you stuck. You can only move or attack when not in any of these four states. And for reference, we'll call this the ready state. The reason why hold commands are so effective, like blocking, jumping and get up options is because you can hold during a stuck state so that the command will activate as soon as you get to ready state. No need to time it. The parry mechanic, on the other hand, is riskier because it must be timed perfectly, especially because it has lots of recovery Frames. So how do you use all these concepts to win? The advantage in a fighting game comes from being in a ready state while your opponent is still in one of the four stuck states, even if it's just by one frame or 50 frames. If you want to be a scientist, you can see the exact amount of frame advantage or disadvantage in training mode. But Tag games like 2xko do not require knowing exact numbers. In most cases, it's much better to know which situations overall put you in advantage or disadvantage. I'm going to give you some common examples so you can get some ideas on how to form your own strategies. At the beginning of the match, neither player has advantage or disadvantage, which is why this situation is called Neutral. The goal is to put yourself at advantage to maximize your chance of landing a hit while not getting hit yourself. The strategies for neutral are endless, but I will start with the most, most basic one. Walk up to your opponent and hit them. If this sounds like a terrible idea, then congratulations, you are thinking strategically. Darius, with his long reach, will chop you long before you get to him. But if Ekko jumps at him right before, Darius would whiff his attack and be stuck in an attack state where he cannot block, Ekko, still in ready state, is free to strike. Hitting someone on their attack's recovery frames like this is called a punishment and a message will pop up. Let's rewind. But this time Darius does a faster attack, allowing him to get to ready state before Ekko reaches him. With both in ready state, there are many possibilities. One. Echo attacks Darius blocks. Blocked jumping attacks give the attacker serious frame advantage because the landing cuts off the attack animation and puts them in ready state immediately. Still stuck in blocks. Then Darius attacking here would be super risky. 2. Echo attacks Darius does Down Heavy. The startup frames of Down Heavy are invincible to air attacks, so it is designed to be what's known as an anti air. If in pulse mode, Mash light to do a combo right after. 3. Darius does down Heavy and Echo Blocks. Advantage can vary here depending on the timing. 4. Both block and have a stare down. This often ends with one throwing the other and putting them in a knockdown state. The forward throw in this game is special because the downed player cannot choose where to get up. Like in normal knockdown situations, this is called a hard knockdown and every champion's throw should give plenty of frame advantage to attack before their opponent can. With the target locked down, the attacker can plan it so the opponent won't know what attack will happen upon getting up, or even bait them Another situation where an attacker can get serious advantage is with lingering projectiles or bombs. This is because by the time the defender gets in block or hitstun, the attacker is already in a ready state able to move around. Echo players love playing keep away while throwing timewinders and dashing in when one makes contact. And Jinx players can even combo hitstun from projectiles from full screen. But the ultimate advantage comes from a tag assist because your point character can be in ready state the entire time. It has the out of body benefits of projectiles, but you can call a tag assist while moving and attacking so they can protect you both on defense and offense and it will let you extend combos. In the earlier example, if Ekko jumped at Darius with a tag assist, it would beat his downforce heavy and you can combo off the hitstun. Yes, it's that good. But be careful. Tag assists can get hit so you risk getting both your champions killed at the same time. Sometimes it's just better to block. This is a lot to absorb, but at least now you know how these games work. If you're ready to break in and give the game a go, here are some good final things to know. Use a controller you are so comfortable with that you can access the button you want immediately. This matters because in fighting games, timing is everything. Almost any controller can be good and the best players do not all use any one type. Just avoid analog sticks for your directionals. If you can avoid it, timing takes time. Ask any musician. In fighting games you are also timing things. Under pressure you will mess up your inputs and die for it, but so will your opponent. Over time this will happen less and you will get better. Take advantage of Pulse, the game's auto combo system. When starting out, finding ways to land the hit that leads to a combo matters more than optimizing combo damage. When you feel ready, you can practice manual combos. These are super satisfying to land in a match. If you don't want to learn two champions now use the Juggernaut or Sidekick Fuse. These are very viable and let you main just one buffed champion. Duo mode lets you play co op with a friend which is fun and eases the pressure. Best of all, winning feels twice as good while losing feels half as bad. Lastly, Don't try to parry everything. That's it. This was Gerald from Core A Gaming thanks for watching."

# essay = "The tale of the British Berserker is one of those stories that feels more like a climax to a movie than an actual historical event. But according to Wikipedia, I looked at other sources. Wikipedia is just easier to read. It was just one of the many pivotal battles between England, led by King Herold ii, against the invading Norwegian Vikings, led by King Herold iii. There are two things about this story that make me want to believe this was someone's creative writing session back in 1060. I don't know when this happened. Number one, the fact they fought on a bridge called Stanford Bridge that led to a town also called Stamford Bridge. Number two, according to documents, the Vikings had no idea that such a large English army was at their doorstep. Most of them were completely drunk and asleep in the middle of the day. So when an entire English army shows up, they overwhelmingly get the advantage in battle. But what halted the English army in their tracks was that on that narrow wooden bridge, one lone warrior, his name unrecorded in history, raised his axe and stood defiant against 15,000 English warriors intent on granting his king and his people the time they desperately needed. And the only other information we know about this guy is that he was labeled a berserker by his enemies for his barbaric way of fighting and the fact that he just wouldn't go down. He made Stamford Bridge a bloodbath, and he managed to take down 40 English soldiers all by himself before someone wised up and stabbed him with a spear from below the bridge. Oh, that's so lame. His story is a story of a man who reached the peak of masculinity. Satan sacrificing himself in combat, and is the kind of man I fantasize in my head as I'm on my way to work. Pretend I'm driving. Okay, I forgot to get actual footage of me driving when I was going to work. So yeah, This scenario my adult brain whipped up comes from the part of my mind that the Internet has deemed to coin as much as my masculine urge. Initially, it was a term people use to ironically make fun of hyper masculine dumb boy things like throwing a rock to break a sheet of ice, or throwing trash into a bin from really far away. Or to wear a long overcoat and wander through the streets during a cold winter night. Or to dig a really big hole. This funny little trend, however, gets kind of interesting when men across the world agree on more serious masculine urges to a pretty noticeable degree. Like the urge to seclude yourself from society to focus on self improvement, being able to perform A task at the highest level due to grueling, meticulous training and of course being able to pull off an extraordinary heroic feat that saves lives. Everywhere I look, if you go down the rabbit hole enough, there are guys all agreeing that being extremely capable at something that benefits the lives of others is a fantasy of theirs. And the apex of this idea is the act of self sacrifice in combat. One of man's biggest fantasies. Now, I am not a neuroscientist, but I am certain that this tickle at the back of my brain when I watch this kind of stuff. It's real, right? It's real. I'm not just concussed, it's a real thing that you guys experience too. I want to preface that this isn't all men. We are not a symbiotic hive mind that think and act the same. But a lot of us are pretty damn close. Search up any YouTube video that's titled something like POV this is your final stand. You'll feel like you just walked into a room full of shonen protagonists. It's kind of cringe, but I love it. And audio. When it's time to lock in this is your villain arc or the all reliable the indomitable human spirit. It's clear from the comments of these videos that people listen to this stuff to get a good pump for a workout or when they need motivation to get back on the metaphoric horse of life. The reason why I bring this up in the context of self sacrifice is these one liners are often taken from scenes where the character unlocks their hidden potential. They cast aside every other desire except for overcoming the obstacle in front of them. And whether they die or not, they're throwing everything they humanly can at you. Because just like every RPG, you can still hit a crit at 1 HP. This then usually follows with the superhuman feat and or going out in a blaze of glory. And a lot of guys resonate with this kind of stuff. Me included. I, along with these Reddit people, fantasize of a world where we hone in solely on the enemy and for a moment become mankind's most dangerous fighter. It's like that one Kanye west song, you know. It's provocative guy. Imagine being someone that powerful. Imagine the satisfaction of being that unit that just doesn't go down. And even if you do die, you're known as the warrior that took down 200 people to kill or something. Such a fantasy would only be seen in the movies, except when it actually did happen in feudal Japan. Saito Musashibo Benkei was a Japanese warrior monk who defended the bridge to his lord's castle to his dying breath. It was said that he sliced and diced over 300 soldiers with his nakiba in order to buy time for his master to honorably commit seppuku. That's 299 times someone has had to watch their guy get eviscerated. And the guy that killed him just stares at you like. Next by number 300 or so, the anime wised up and was like, oh yeah, screw this. And started taking pot shots at him from a distance. And he still tanks them. After riddling him with so many arrows, one brave soldier actually gets close enough to look at him, only to realize that he was already dead. He was already gone multiple arrows ago. And he died standing upright with his glaive in his hand, propping himself up. This is literally Whitebeard. The thing is, this guy was basically already an anime character before all this. He's a weapons expert who KE his Masakari, his Kumade, his Tetsubo, and a bunch of other weapons on him at all times. He roamed Japan, claiming the swords of over 999 different samurai he had defeated. And he would have gotten to number 1000 if it wasn't for that damn Yitzune who beat him in combat. But it was that same Yitzune that became his best buddy for life and was the same guy he defended on that bridge. Now, I'm gonna refrain from speaking about this any longer. Cause I can feel that masculine tingle in my head and it's happening again, and I don't know how to stop it. That epic feeling of someone's life in your hands and the odds of survival being so close to zero ironically fills you up with adrenaline to keep on going. At least I think it does. The closest thing I ever got to Mortal Kombat was when I was clutching a Victory Royale with the boys. Benkei, Whitebeard, all might Moomin Rider, Future Gohan. It might be a little cringe, but I identify with these people. I identify with the virtue of self sacrifice because it shows that you are willing to risk your life for something that is bigger than just your sentiment of wanting to stay alive. Well, why is it self sacrifice specifically that I like? That same courageous virtue could definitely be seen with moms raising children or a child taking care of their parents in their old age. The short answer is that would be a terrible action movie to watch. A long answer, we're gonna have to enter a pretty dark and malevolent place to get it. A place full of toxic COD lobbies and men hating on any woman that isn't built like an over sexualized white 90s character. A place I never truly understood. But for the sake of the video, and I love you guys, we're gonna open this Pandora's box to make sense of a few things. I'm talking about the world of masculinity. Hey, y' all like the fit despite the cat ears? This is the most masculine thing I could come up with. My masculine outfit is in the wash until Tuesday, trust me. Okay, but the lumberjack outfit I thought exuded masculinity. All right, you blue haired betas. Oh God, my eyes. Class is in session. Not doing that again, despite my machismo. Look, we're not here to talk about sports or cars or women. We're here to talk about gender theory. Gender theory. Now look, I decided to get a degree in something that society actually values and pays decently well. So I am not exactly an expert in this whole debacle, but I am trying my best here. What you are witnessing now is a live autopsy of a guy analyzing his past self and why he had these kind of red pill adjacent ideas about masculinity. I'm just hoping we can all learn from it. Okay, I'm not exactly prideful or happy I used to think this way, but hey, we thank God for progress. Okay, so if I slip up and say something out of pocket. That's why gender euphoria is simply defined as the satisfaction someone feels when their gendered experience aligns with their gendered identity. Basically, it feels good to be a boy and do boy stuff because you're taught this is what boys like. We all basically know this. But there are certain traits as a man that feel good to perform as a man who follows these ideals of what a man is supposed to be. These are some of them now. Oh, okay. Yeah. Okay. Yeah. Some of these got updated in the last patch, so I don't. I don't know why they're still there. Traits like self sufficiency, dominance, being powerful and muscular. These are the things that a guy who falls under this sense of what it means to be a man values in a sense, because in their head, that is what's necessary in order to perform as a man. And I want to put heavy emphasis on the term perform because it actually is a performance. There are definitely times as a man where I don't want to be all these things, but I feel like I gotta hence performing. It's Not a natural thing. In fact, the male body might compel us to cry or be vulnerable at certain times, but as a man, we don't cry, right? So we thug it out, you know, we thug it out and then we die at 60, you know. Despite the discourse online that certain men say that men don't care about feelings and they're not important to us, I think they're very much are. Look at these red pillars. When you encroach on their identity as a man, what they think it means to be a man, and then you'll see yourself if feelings are definitely not important for a man or never are expressed properly. Authenticity defined by Charles Taylor is the creation and construction as well as discovery of oneself. To be authentic is to perform these traits you think aligns with you order to become a man. So this is what they hammer in. This is the appeal for a lot of self help advice for men online. There's a road you're supposed to follow and if you follow it, you'll come out the other end a man, an authentic man. Traditional masculinity will tell you that you are not just born a man, but you need to become one as well. This is a big reason why a lot of male dominated media usually have this stoic powerhouse of a man moving as a force of nature, pummeling enemies into submission. And then we as men all go, yeah, yeah, that guy has it all figured out. I need to be like him. So researching this video, I discovered a pretty spooky place on Reddit. There was this post about asking these veterans from war why they like war so much. And despite how unpleasant and traumatic so many other soldiers say it is, why do they specifically almost like obsess over it and dream about it like it's the glory days? Here are some of my favorite quotes and responses to that question. War stops time and intensifies the experience to the point of horrific ecstasy. War brings infinite foreign experiences and a lifetime's worth of unbelievable. There's just something about knowing you won't make it that helps you fight so much harder. And we all, and men in particular, still want and need a narrative. All right, yeah, someone take Master Chief's keyboard away. This guy is yapping too much. Guy you. Did you just hear how metal that sounded? We might discuss it with our friends and make these long intellectual